Upstream: none found (GitLab search 2026-11-21); closed issue sequoia-pgp/sequoia#1011 made
Hash::hash fallible, and Key4::fingerprint still expects it to succeed.

Key4::fingerprint hashes the key packet body (version, creation time, algorithm and public key
material) with a two-octet length, and Key4::hash fails with "number too large to fit in target
type" when the body is longer than 65535 octets. fingerprint() expects the hash to succeed, so it
panics. Key4::parse accepted such keys: the public key material of an unknown or private
algorithm is kept as is, up to the end of the packet (known algorithms stay far below the limit).
Cert::from_bytes computes the fingerprint when it builds the certificate, so a public key packet
of 65.5 KB panics cert_from_bytes. The message_from_bytes input this was found with formatted such
a key with {:?} (which includes the fingerprint) in the "Unexpected body data" error of
PacketParser::buffer_unread_content; its oversized key material came from the bug that
fix-partial-body-consume-bound.patch fixes. Reject the key packet in Key4::parse instead; it is
then returned as an Unknown packet.

openpgp/tests/key4_public_len.rs parses v4 public keys with 65529, 65530 and 70000 octets of key
material. Without the patch it panics in Key4::fingerprint (x86_64 and i686); with it the first
parses as a key and the others as Unknown packets.

